<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bai Qi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 257 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Lord Wu'an</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bai Qi was a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> and one of the most successful commanders of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He defeated Han and Wei at Yique in 293 BCE, captured the Chu capital Ying in 278 BCE and destroyed the army of Zhao at Changping in 260 BCE, where captured soldiers were reportedly massacred. After quarrelling with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> court he was ordered to commit suicide.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bai-qi.jpg" alt="Bai Qi" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bai_Qi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bai Qi, also known as Bo Qi and Gongsun Qi, was born around 332 BCE in Mei, in present-day Mei County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. Later writers gave him different ancestries: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a> traced him to Baiyi Bing, a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-mu-of-qin">Duke Mu of Qin</a>, while the Tang poet Bai Juyi claimed descent from Baigong Sheng, a grandson of King Ping of Chu. He commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> army for more than 30 years under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-zhaoxiang-of-qin">King Zhaoxiang of Qin</a>, who promoted him to the rank of Da Liang Zao in 292 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His campaigns were marked by mass killing. At Yique in 293 BCE his army reportedly slaughtered around 240,000 enemy soldiers, and at the capture of Ying in 278 BCE he reportedly drowned 100,000 people in a flood attack, earning the title Lord Wu'an. In 273 BCE he defeated Zhao and Wei at Huayang and massacred about 150,000 soldiers who had submitted. In 264 BCE he took five Han fortresses and beheaded 50,000 men.</p><p>At Changping in 260 BCE he replaced Wang He as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> commander after Zhao had put the inexperienced Zhao Kuo in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lian-po">Lian Po</a>. He split the Zhao army and cut its supply lines and retreat; after Zhao Kuo was shot dead, more than 400,000 Zhao soldiers who had surrendered were buried alive on his orders. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> he seized more than 73 cities, and no record shows him losing a battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bai Qi wished to finish Zhao, but the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-ju">Fan Ju</a>, fearing his power, persuaded the king to halt and negotiate. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a> in 257 BCE, Bai Qi pleaded illness, argued that Qin lacked the resources for a long war and refused command several times while the siege failed under Wang Ling and Wang He. The angry king stripped his titles, expelled him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> and, persuaded by Fan Ju that he might serve another state, forced him to kill himself at Duyou.</p><p>Before dying Bai Qi said he deserved his end for killing so many. Known as Ren Tu, the human butcher, he is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> more as a symbol of brutality than of military talent, and human remains are still found at Changping.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bai Qi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yet it suffered major defeats: Qin took Hanzhong in 312 BCE, and the Qin general <b>Bai Qi</b> captured Ying in 278 BCE and burned the royal tombs at Yiling.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bai Qi</b> defeated Han and Wei at Yique in 293 BCE and captured Ying, the capital of Chu, in 278 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mei County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.27,107.74&amp;year=-267" aria-label="Mei County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bai Qi"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4140 4589h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4140 4589h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.27,107.74&amp;year=-267">What was here in 267 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
